From b30fd96e0ac27572372fdc9be20d73103ffab347 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:29:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/22] fix(budget): a gateway key with no credit limit is
 blocked unless explicitly unlimited

Bug: credit_limit_usd <= 0 on an Alpharouter API key meant "no cap", so a
key created with the field left empty (the UI hint even said "0 also means
no cap") could spend without any per-period ceiling, and nothing in the
admin list distinguished "unlimited on purpose" from "forgotten".

Change:
- alpha_router_api_keys.unlimited_budget (Boolean, nullable so layer-2
  schema patching can add it). ensure_key_usable answers 402 "no credit
  limit configured" when the limit is <= 0 and the flag is not set; every
  gateway request and reservation goes through it.
- Admin API: create refuses limit 0 without unlimited_budget=true; patch
  refuses leaving a key with neither. key_to_dict and the audit changelog
  carry the flag.
- db_migrate.backfill_api_key_unlimited_budget maps rows created before the
  column (NULL) from the old semantics once: no positive limit -> true,
  otherwise false. Existing keys keep working; admins can now see which are
  unlimited. Called from app.migrate and the lifespan after column patches.
- UI: "Unlimited budget" checkbox with a warning; the list shows
  "Unlimited" or "Blocked (no limit set)" instead of a dash.

Verified on PostgreSQL 16: dropped the column, seeded legacy rows, ran
python -m app.migrate -> column added, nocap row true, capped row false.

Tests: tests/test_api_key_unlimited_budget.py (402 vs unlimited vs capped,
admin create/patch validation, backfill mapping); test fixtures that relied
on the implicit no-cap now say unlimited_budget=True.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UTnpHajSmkk2JMLfuQoV7o
